From: Alan Shearer
Subject: arp: do not dereference a NULL protocol when flushing the resolve queue

Packets waiting for a MAC are queued on the arp_entry and sent from
MarkValid() once the mapping is known.

protocol is only set in arp_start_resolve() and arp_set_local_entry().
arp_update_entry() can MarkValid() an entry learned from a request or
reply whose protocol pointer is still NULL. send_data() through
protocol->next is then a NULL dereference (KDL).

If the pointer is missing, drop the queue. If send_data() fails, the
ethernet frame module does not take the buffer — free it, same as the
ARP timer already does for request templates.

---
 src/add-ons/kernel/network/datalink_protocols/arp/arp.cpp | 16 ++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/add-ons/kernel/network/datalink_protocols/arp/arp.cpp b/src/add-ons/kernel/network/datalink_protocols/arp/arp.cpp
--- a/src/add-ons/kernel/network/datalink_protocols/arp/arp.cpp
+++ b/src/add-ons/kernel/network/datalink_protocols/arp/arp.cpp
@@ -337,16 +337,24 @@
 
 	flags = (flags & ~ARP_FLAG_REJECT) | ARP_FLAG_VALID;
 
+	if (protocol == NULL || protocol->next == NULL) {
+		ClearQueue();
+		return;
+	}
+
 	BufferList::Iterator iterator = queue.GetIterator();
 	while (iterator.HasNext()) {
 		net_buffer *buffer = iterator.Next();
 		iterator.Remove();
 
 		TRACE(("  ARP Dequeing packet %p...\n", buffer));
 
 		memcpy(buffer->destination, &hardware_address,
 			hardware_address.sdl_len);
-		protocol->next->module->send_data(protocol->next, buffer);
+		status_t status = protocol->next->module->send_data(protocol->next,
+			buffer);
+		if (status < B_OK)
+			gBufferModule->free(buffer);
 	}
 }
 
